from pathlib import Path
r=Path('outputs/SpiritAshResummon')
p=r/'README.md';s=p.read_text();start=s.index('## Requirements and current compatibility');s='''# SpiritAshResummon 0.2.0 — multiple-type diagnostic prototype

A standalone Rust DLL for offline, single-player Elden Ring. Different Spirit Ash types can coexist, with one group per type. Reusing an Ash heals only its own surviving members by 25% of each member's maximum HP. If every member of that type is gone, reuse requests a fresh group.

**0.2.0 compiles but has not been tested in game.** Earlier 0.1.12 observations do not establish that the new multi-type paths work. All gameplay and automated test execution is left to the user. Boss-fight resummoning remains not attempted; the user chose to move on after bosses died too quickly.

## Intended behavior

- Summon different Ash types together. Upgrade variants count as the same type, preventing duplicates through different upgrade levels.
- Reuse one living type to heal that type's survivors only, with normal FP/HP cost once. Other types remain untouched; dead individual members are not revived.
- Full-health reuse is a no-op. Entirely dead types can be resummoned without dismissing other types.
- Normal locations, animation, scaling, resource requirements and native area/death/boss cleanup remain required. No resource subtraction is implemented in the DLL.
- No configurable Ash-type count limit. A bounded native pool provides **500 Spirit actor slots** (mounted actors may consume more than one slot). Requests that cannot fit the whole group are rejected. This is a technical ceiling, not literal infinite allocation; high-count performance is untested.
- No summon-anywhere, damage/defense/base-HP changes, passive regeneration, HUD changes, follower warps, save editing or regulation.bin editing.

'''+s[start:]
s=s.replace('Expect successful verification of all eight call sites and a BuddyStone row count.','Expect `Startup patches installed: call_sites=10, multi_type=true, actor_pool=520`, then `Game task ready` and a BuddyStone row count. The 520-entry registry reserves its first 20 slots; 500 are available to Spirits.')
s=s.replace('Defaults are resummoning/healing enabled, 25%, logging off.','Defaults are multiple types/resummoning/healing enabled, 25%, logging off. `AllowMultipleAshTypes=false` selects the legacy single-type behavior and omits the capacity/spawn-retention patches.')
s=s.replace('Setting `HealOnReuse=false` keeps native same-Ash dismissal, while the one-type restriction remains.','With multiple types enabled, `HealOnReuse=false` rejects living-Ash reuse to avoid dismissing unrelated groups. In legacy single-type mode it keeps native same-Ash dismissal.')
s=s.replace('`Build.ps1` runs tests, builds the release DLL, and stages `dist/`; it does not install or launch the mod.','`Build.ps1` builds and stages a separate package without running tests or changing the installed `dist/`. Use `-RunTests` only when you explicitly want to execute automated tests. It does not install or launch the mod.')
p.write_text(s)
p=r/'IMPLEMENTATION.md';old=p.read_text();old=old.replace('# Implementation and technical plan','# Historical architecture through 0.1.12',1)
p.write_text('''# Implementation — 0.2.0

## Scope change and status

The user requested coexistence of different Ash types on 2026-10-29, superseding the original single-type restriction. This build is compiled, not runtime validated. The historical sections below record earlier architecture and diagnostics; this section defines current multi-type behavior.

## Group identity and healing

`src/multi.rs` is included by `runtime.rs` and reuses its ownership, registry, handle, health-module and commit-time page checks. The selected Goods row supplies the trigger effect. `policy::unique_family` resolves exactly one base effect within upgrade offsets 0–10, gathers all linked BuddyParam IDs and rejects ambiguous/overlapping families. Only local-owner entries with those IDs can become healing targets. Upgrade levels share one type identity; unrelated groups never become targets. Existing characters retain their original stats and upgrade effects.

A surviving selected member selects Heal (or Reject when everyone is full/HealOnReuse is off). No surviving selected member selects AddGroup. Unknown lifecycle states reject rather than count as death. Exiting entries in the observed 2/2 or 4/2 registry states can be ignored without dereferencing them; these entries are never healed. A missing/unknown registry entry remains ambiguous and rejects. Skeleton revival transitions are still a known testing gap.

Healing retains the 0.1.10/0.1.12 typed HP implementation: `min(max, current + floor(max * percentage / 100))`. Fresh page/write and unchanged HP/max checks precede all writes. No actor pointers persist across callbacks. Reuse never invokes native spawning. Matching a type no longer depends on the game's single last-active-Goods field.

## Native activation and resource costs

The original eight item/requirement/cost/effect hooks remain. Native state queried with Goods=-1 supplies active-group readiness; the selected Goods-specific -1 can mean merely a different type from the game's last summon. The new policy may replace that Goods-specific rejection only after independently identifying the selected family, validating native active state 0/1/2, normal activation range, absence of manager dismissal/pending request and expiration of the native cooldown. Native active state -1 is never changed to ready. Item-area, multiplayer and requirement functions remain unpatched.

For AddGroup, the mod calls native DoSummon once. A thread-local scope applies only during that synchronous call. Its internal state call at RVA `0x4b86a9` can report ready instead of active/dismissal for the validated request. It does not broadly change GetBuddyState or schedule a second request. Native origin/scaling/spawn checks remain in DoSummon.

A bounded pending token stores only world/manager identity addresses and owner/Goods/effect values, never actor references. The spawn-specific clear call at `0x4b8a90` normally invokes `0x4bba80` to erase every owner group before adding the new one. For the exact accepted pending request, this one clear call is suppressed, preserving other groups. Other cleanup call sites are untouched; disappearance flags/timers are never reset. The token is discarded when current world/player/manager/request no longer matches. No delayed replay or synthetic request is implemented.

Cost wrappers expose normal paid state for a validated Heal/AddGroup. While the exact accepted AddGroup is still pending, the matching cost query also exposes paid state so it cannot become a free-dismissal calculation midway through consumption. The engine owns affordability and deduction; there is no manual FP/HP subtraction or custom price. Exact-once timing, interrupted activations and HP-cost Ashes require user-run validation in 0.2.0.

## Actor capacity and startup transaction

`src/capacity.rs` adapts upstream `engine_patches.rs::spirit_set_size` and `spirit_slots`. The constructor epilogue at `0x495675` expands only the just-constructed empty 80-entry registry to 520 zeroed entries through the same native allocator/free pair. The entry initializer at `0x3c3810` was inspected: zeroed storage gives its empty pointer/status/flag state. The original allocation and initialization body is unchanged, avoiding mixed allocation/loop sizes during installation. The complete pinned constructor bytes are checked first. Allocation failure leaves the old set; the gameplay adapter refuses an unexpected capacity.

The slot-start sequence at `0x493492` begins at 20. The bounded search at `0x4934c0` uses actual capacity and wraps once, retaining native full-pool/found-slot exits. New groups preflight available slots using all family BuddyParam rows and their mount requirements; no existing group is evicted. There is no per-type-count cap, but memory remains finite: 500 Spirit body slots. No stress/performance claim is made for large counts.

Startup checks WW file version 2.7.2.0, known conflicting DLLs and all original bytes. Ten call-site redirects and three capacity/search redirects are prepared as one transaction, then peer threads are suspended, byte ranges rechecked, writes committed or rolled back, and peers resumed. Thread overlap checks now cover variable-length sites. Stubs are RX before installation. Hooks are installed early enough for registry construction, but gameplay callbacks pass through until the game task publishes readiness. A late-loaded player or wrong registry capacity disables gameplay adaptation; restart is required. No live registry resize, hot unload or online compatibility is supported. A disabled adapter does not uninstall already-applied code patches; exit restores the process.

## Files and compatibility

New: `src/multi.rs`, `src/capacity.rs`. Updated: `runtime.rs`, `hooks.rs`, `threads.rs`, `policy.rs`, `config.rs`, `lib.rs`, Cargo manifests/lock, configuration, build/preflight tooling and documentation. `AllowMultipleAshTypes=true` is optional and defaults on. False keeps the legacy single-type policy and eight hooks, without capacity or spawn-retention patches.

Upstream unrestricted-area/item/range patches, item-limit parameter clearing, low-FP/free dismissal and group flag/timer resets are deliberately excluded. Existing native item restrictions may expose additional compatibility gaps; unknown modded parameter families fail closed. Version signatures and source inspection cannot establish live safety. Loading the DLL with another summon-system mod, Seamless Co-op or protected online play is unsupported. MIT/Apache and dependency notices remain in the package.

---

'''+old)
p=r/'VALIDATION.md';s=p.read_text();s=s.replace('# Validation record and user-operated test guide\n','# Validation record and user-operated test guide\n\n## 0.2.0 multi-type prototype — pending user validation\n\nThe user moved on from boss resummoning after another boss died too quickly. That case remains NOT ATTEMPTED. The original different-Ash rejection requirement is replaced by one group per unique Ash type. Earlier 0.1.12 results remain historical and do not validate 0.2.0.\n\nRelease compilation for x86_64-pc-windows-msvc succeeded (`evidence/build-0.2.0.txt`). Static source/instruction inspection identified the native spawn clear call, internal state call, constructor and slot search. REA deep decompilation was unavailable; local pefile/Capstone inspection supplied the instruction evidence. No automated tests, DLL execution probes or gameplay tests were run for this update, per user instructions. New pure-policy test cases are supplied but unexecuted.\n\n### First user-run session\n\n1. Launch the existing offline test profile. Load Gatefront and wait 15 seconds without summoning; report FPS and leave the game open for startup-log review. Stop here if there is a crash, slowdown or disabled-adapter message.\n2. Summon Lone Wolf Ashes once. Expect three wolves and 55 FP spent. Refill to 91 FP.\n3. While wolves remain alive, use Skeletal Militiaman Ashes once. Expect two Militiamen alongside the wolves, 44 FP spent (91 -> 47), no dismissal and no duplicate wolves. Wait 15 seconds, report counts/FPS/FP, and request log review.\n4. After the coexistence log is reviewed, injure both types and refill FP. Use Wolves once: only surviving wolves should heal, costing 55 FP. Refill and use Militiamen once: only their surviving members should heal, costing 44 FP. Record counts and HP/cost observations separately.\n\n### Follow-up cases (not yet run)\n\n- Repeat either Ash at full health: no charge, dismissal or additional members.\n- Attempt a heal or a new type with insufficient resources: no effect/charge. HP-cost Ashes require separate testing.\n- Kill one member of one type: heal its survivors without reviving it or affecting the other type.\n- Let an entire type die while another survives: resummon only the dead type, normal cost once, others retained. Repeat with each type and verify no delay/duplicates.\n- Try a different upgrade of an already-active Ash if available: heals its existing group; does not create a second group or change current stats.\n- Add more distinct types in stages, including more than ten total bodies to exercise expanded slots. Monitor counts, FPS and logs; do not jump straight to a stress test.\n- Leave the summoning area, reload and die with several types active, as separate cases: normal cleanup, fresh groups on eligible return and no orphaned entities. Boss cleanup must also be retested.\n- Test interrupted casting, rapid item switching and partially loading/retiring groups. Unknown transitions must not spawn duplicates.\n\nThere is a 500-body pool safety ceiling, not infinite engine capacity. High-count stability, native delayed-spawn behavior, item-limit compatibility and exact-once cost ordering remain unproven. Boss resummoning remains deferred at user request.\n\n---\n\n',1);p.write_text(s)
p=r/'CHANGELOG.md';s=p.read_text().replace('# Changelog\n','# Changelog\n\n## 0.2.0 — 2026-10-29 (multiple distinct Ash types; untested prototype)\n\n- User-authorized scope change: one group per Ash type, with different types allowed together; upgrade variants share a type.\n- Match healing targets to the selected Ash family independently of the native last-active item. Keep existing health/cost safeguards.\n- Add a scoped internal native-state hook and exact-request spawn-clear guard to retain other groups. No timer resets or delayed request replay.\n- Adapt upstream constructor/slot-search expansion to 500 Spirit body slots, with signature checks and capacity rejection.\n- Install the startup patch transaction before registry construction; keep gameplay gated on game-task readiness.\n- Add optional AllowMultipleAshTypes setting and policy test source. Compile release DLL; automated and in-game tests not executed.\n- Boss resummoning remains not attempted and is deferred at user request.\n\n',1);p.write_text(s)
p=r/'UPSTREAM.md';s=p.read_text().replace('Relevant upstream sources:','0.2.0 additionally adapts the constructor-epilogue allocator/free stub and bounded shared-slot search from `src/engine_patches.rs`, plus the spawn-specific owner-clear interception concept and internal DoSummon state address. The new family policy and scoped pending-request authorization replace upstream\'s broader multi-summon behavior. Original notices apply to these adaptations.\n\nRelevant upstream sources:');p.write_text(s)
